---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="05-lists"
  lessonSlug="03-list-methods"
  hook="Think Python's most-quoted Debugging warning: don't write `t = t.sort()`. sort() returns None."
>
  <h2>The trap</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="The classic mistake"
    code={`t = [3, 1, 2]
t = t.sort()              # ← BUG: sort() returns None
print(t)                  # None
# Then your next operation on t crashes...`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python ch.10 Debugging">
    <p>
      <em>"Don't forget that most list methods modify the argument and return
      None. This is the opposite of the string methods, which return a new
      string and leave the original alone."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The fix — `sorted()` and `list(reversed())`</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def sorted_copy(t):
    return sorted(t)

def reversed_copy(t):
    return list(reversed(t))            # reversed() returns an iterator

nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]
print("Sorted  :", sorted_copy(nums))
print("Reversed:", reversed_copy(nums))
print("Original:", nums, "(unchanged)")`}
  />

  <h2>The full cheat-sheet</h2>
  <table class="text-sm">
    <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>In-place (mutates)</th><th>New list (preserves)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sort</td><td><code>t.sort()</code></td><td><code>sorted(t)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reverse</td><td><code>t.reverse()</code></td><td><code>list(reversed(t))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Append</td><td><code>t.append(x)</code></td><td><code>t + [x]</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Remove last</td><td><code>t.pop()</code></td><td><code>t[:-1]</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>When in doubt, prefer the right column — they're safer.</p>
</Lecture>
